I'm not much of a source diver, but:

struct permonst *
courtmon()
{
	int     i = rn2(60) + rn2(3*level_difficulty());
	if (i > 100)		return(mkclass(S_DRAGON,0));
	else if (i > 95)	return(mkclass(S_GIANT,0));
	else if (i > 85)	return(mkclass(S_TROLL,0));
	else if (i > 75)	return(mkclass(S_CENTAUR,0));
	else if (i > 60)	return(mkclass(S_ORC,0));
	else if (i > 45)	return(&mons[PM_BUGBEAR]);
	else if (i > 30)	return(&mons[PM_HOBGOBLIN]);
	else if (i > 15)	return(mkclass(S_GNOME,0));
	else			return(mkclass(S_KOBOLD,0));
}

Thrones *can* generate dragons, based on level difficulty. Level
difficulty is equal to your current level if you do not have the
amulet, or the deepest level reached (the sanctum) if you have the
amulet. With this equation, it's impossible to generate dragons at
thrones higher than DLVL 15, unless you have the amulet. The maximum
depth of the main dungeon is 25-30, and thrones cannot be generated in
Gehenom. Without the amulet, the approximate probabilities per attempt
at a given depth are, by my calculation:
DLVL 15 = 0.8%
DLVL 20 = 6.4%
DLVL 25 = 14.8%
DLVL 30 = 25.6%

With the amulet, max depth can be from 50-60, so regardless of your
current dungeon level, any throne has a probability of generating a
dragon ranging from 69.1% to 92.7%, depending on the depth of the
sanctum.

Brief wizmode testing on DLVL 1 with the amulet and having visited the
sanctum at DLVL 52 seems in line with my calculations. I was getting
dragons roughly 70% of the time. Nevertheless, someone who's taken a
statistics course recently might want to check my math :P



